00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:55.750
and get this to kind of snowball
into something that's going

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:57.970
to be much bigger than just us.

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:00.160
So the Indaba [INAUDIBLE]
really encompasses

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.920
that, in that we're trying
to strengthen African machine

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:03.770
learning.

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:08.970
And by doing that, it's
building the community in itself

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:10.720
as one way of doing that.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:15.550
Learning from each other,
offering each other's skills

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:18.400
to the community,
and seeing where

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:21.100
this is going to get us in
kind of a couple of years.

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:22.880
So even though this
is the second year,

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:24.580
this is still like
the beginning.

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:25.220
MELANIE WARRICK:
And you mentioned

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:27.520
that it's the second year,
last year being the first year.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:28.870
Where was last year,
and how many people?

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:30.900
VUKOSI MARIVATE: Last
year was at the University

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:31.810
of Witwatersrand.

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:33.910
That university has its
home in Johannesburg.

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:36.394
And that was about
330 people from 22

00:10:36.394 --> 00:10:37.560
different African countries.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:37.930
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:10:37.930 --> 00:10:40.460
And this year it's around
550, is my understanding.

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:41.376
VUKOSI MARIVATE: Yeah.

00:10:41.376 --> 00:10:42.100
550, as a target.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:43.060
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:44.770
Well, when you
mentioned community,

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:47.228
I know we were talking actually
before we started recording

00:10:47.228 --> 00:10:49.540
about how there's all
these Deep Learning IndabaX

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:51.659
events and community
building activities

00:10:51.659 --> 00:10:53.950
that have been happening
throughout different countries

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:55.180
within Africa.

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:58.030
And so Yasin, I know you
are one of the organizers.

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:00.654
And specifically you
organized one in Zambia.

00:11:00.654 --> 00:11:02.320
And when was that
that you organized it?

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:03.750
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: On
the 24th of March.

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:04.370
MELANIE WARRICK: OK.

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:07.090
And what was that like, in terms
of organizing the event there?

00:11:07.090 --> 00:11:08.080
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: It
was a bit challenging,

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.390
you know, organizing
the IndabaX in Zambia.

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:13.840
Because one was the
issue of resources.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:17.710
Having spent most of my time
in South Africa and organizing

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:20.260
things here, I was
assuming that it was going

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:21.550
to be the same that I did.

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:23.380
But when I got
there, I literally

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:26.890
had to pay for everything,
including venues, computers

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:28.280
and things like that.

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:31.150
So when we kind of
advertised for people

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:33.760
to kind of register
for the IndabaX,

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:35.890
we received a number
of applications.

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:38.920
But sadly, due to the
number of resources,

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:40.950
we could only
accommodate a few in it.

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:43.720
We only accommodated
about 60 participants.

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:45.720
And I had participants
[INAUDIBLE]

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:47.800
when we could not
accommodate them.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:52.000
And mostly, if you look at the
expenses that were incurred,

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.569
you know, there's expenses
that were not supposed

00:11:54.569 --> 00:11:55.610
to be incurred, in a way.

00:11:55.610 --> 00:11:57.700
Because you're
looking at something

00:11:57.700 --> 00:11:59.320
that you want to
empower people, you

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:01.540
want to give
knowledge to people,

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:04.090
you want to produce a
cohort of youth that

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:05.800
are technologically empowered.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:09.820
So we're trying to associate
it to universities.

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.790
But when we approached
universities,

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:16.120
they were not open to the idea,
because they were looking at it

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:17.660
from a marketing perspective.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:20.160
I mean, they were talking
about issues to do with, OK,

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.090
if you want to
partner with us, come

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:24.220
with at least 50 participants.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:28.180
So that, in a way, was
difficult from our end.

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:32.510
Because we couldn't like
provide for the 150 people.

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:37.000
So we ended up like resorting to
having other venues, which was

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:38.539
not supposed to be the case.

00:12:38.539 --> 00:12:40.705
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, what
went well for the event?

00:12:40.705 --> 00:12:41.350
YASIN MUSA AYAMI:
OK, so what went

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:43.630
well is the positive
feedback we got from people.

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:45.760
So people now are
saying, let's make

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:48.100
this an annual thing, whether
Deep Learning Indaba is in

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:50.380
or Deep Learning
Indaba is not in.

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:52.630
So we've got also
some good responses

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:55.330
from corporate people,
and we're hoping that more

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:56.980
would buy into the idea.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:59.740
So like we've had people,
you know, coming to us

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:02.009
and saying, OK, why you
never reached out to us?

00:13:02.009 --> 00:13:02.800
And we're like, no.

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:05.050
The people that you've
employed to kind of let

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:07.660
us have access to you were
denying us access to you.

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:10.600
Or sometimes we'd send
emails, but you're not

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:11.860
responding to our emails.

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:14.440
But at least now we've
kind of established

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:16.030
contact with these
people, and be

00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:17.990
able to contact them directly.

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:21.190
So going forward, I think
the subsequent Indabas

00:13:21.190 --> 00:13:23.860
will be bigger and
better, and obviously

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:26.460
will have more and more
people coming on board.

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:29.920
So the next Indaba, we're
hoping to have at least maybe

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:31.960
150 or more people.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:32.890
And yeah.

00:13:32.890 --> 00:13:35.130
So arising from the
Deep Learning Indaba,

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:38.540
there are some
monthly tutorials that

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:43.930
aim at increasing the
number of people that are

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:46.250
in this area of data science.

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:49.060
And yeah, so we're hoping,
that as the population

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:54.490
grows of people in data
science, then certain issues--

00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:56.300
you can have a number
of people working

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:59.422
on various issues that are
being affected in the country.

00:13:59.422 --> 00:14:00.880
MELANIE WARRICK:
What's going well?

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:01.930
And what are some
of the challenges

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:04.221
that you've seen, Vukosi,
from working on Deep Learning

00:14:04.221 --> 00:14:04.780
Indaba?

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:06.340
VUKOSI MARIVATE: The biggest
challenge is always demand.

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:08.840
MELANIE WARRICK: Very common
to what Yasin's saying, yeah.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:10.000
VUKOSI MARIVATE:
Yeah, starting off

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:12.460
I think, maybe going back to
the beginning of the Indaba,

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:14.895
we had envisioned I think
50 even in the first year?

00:14:14.895 --> 00:14:15.770
MELANIE WARRICK: Wow.

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:17.978
VUKOSI MARIVATE: That was
where we were planning for,

00:14:17.978 --> 00:14:21.359
and we ended up getting
like 700 applications.

00:14:21.359 --> 00:14:22.900
And by then I think
we were thinking,

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:24.160
OK, let's just make it 200.

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:27.250
So by the time 700 applications
came in, it was, we can do 200.

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:29.170
We need to increase this
as much as possible.

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:32.720
So it's something that we
have to come to terms with.

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:34.717
And again, it's
this thing of saying

00:14:34.717 --> 00:14:35.800
that things are happening.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:39.160
People are wanting to
do more in this area.

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:43.000
The amount of times you talk
to professionals, students,

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:46.010
even academics, who
come up and say, look,

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:50.672
yeah, I'm doing y or x, but
I'm actually very interested

00:14:50.672 --> 00:14:52.630
in machine learning and
artificial intelligence

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:55.270
because I have this
idea, and I think

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:57.520
if I can just grasp
the methods, I

00:14:57.520 --> 00:14:59.950
will be able to actually
do something with it.

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:02.020
That's what the Indaba is for.

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:05.590
And the kind of knock
ons are still something

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:08.710
that I think we're still
having a very hard time being

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:10.250
able to quantify.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:12.530
So just from what Yasini
is saying, of saying,

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:14.440
now you have monthly events.

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:17.550
And every time you meet
the IndabaX organizers,

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:20.490
there is always a story
about this other thing

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:23.250
that we actually started
doing afterwards.

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:28.590
And being able to empower them
to continue into their journeys

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:29.460
is also important.

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:33.480
Because you know, we as
the main Indaba organizers,

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:34.440
can only do so much.

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:36.930
You know, you know you
can only cover so many.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.000
So even with the
first year only having

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.050
330 being at the main
event in Johannesburg,

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:46.080
the IndabaXs actually then
had a total over 1,000

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:47.740
over that March
and April period.

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:50.250
So you can see that
that's a bigger

00:15:50.250 --> 00:15:51.730
factor in terms of doing that.

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:54.030
And yes, it does
bring in more people--

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:56.070
more demand, again, to
us, because then people

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:57.040
want to come for the--

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:59.123
MELANIE WARRICK: But that's
a good problem, right?

00:15:59.123 --> 00:16:01.530
VUKOSI MARIVATE: Yeah, that
is a good problem there.

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:04.170
And yes, for us we try
to keep the mission

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:05.880
being the main thing,
the strengthening

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:07.680
of African machine
learning as being what

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:09.150
we want people to sign on for.

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:13.500
So in working to get sponsors
or get partners maybe, you know,

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:15.790
we want them to buy
into that mission.

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:18.420
And if it's not the thing
that they want to do,

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:20.310
it's like-- you
know, we understand.

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:22.980
But it's this cornerstone
that we need to keep,

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:25.260
because that's why
people come here.

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:27.960
They set over six days
and with little sleep

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:31.230
going through a lot of
content, talking to each other.

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:35.320
Like, you get amazed at kind
of some of the connections that

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:36.090
are made.

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:37.250
MELANIE WARRICK: A
significant amount of contact

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:39.150
that's definitely being
covered at this conference,

00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:40.150
so I can understand that.

00:16:40.150 --> 00:16:42.149
And the fact that there's
monthly tutorials that

00:16:42.149 --> 00:16:44.770
are also being produced is
also kind of incredible.

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:46.380
And frankly, I
heard someone saying

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:50.010
how they were amazed at how
much people are just engaged.

00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:52.590
It's so wonderful to see how
much engagement there is,

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:55.540
how much excitement there is
about what's being shared.

00:16:55.540 --> 00:16:56.200
So thank you.

00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:56.700
Well, OK.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:59.130
So let's get into
the fact that there's

00:16:59.130 --> 00:17:02.250
two awards that were given
out, and some honorable

00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:04.000
mentions in relation
to those award.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:06.780
And I know the Maathai Award
in particular was given out,

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:10.020
and it was really to recognize
along the lines of, you know,

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:12.420
even people who are
individuals, especially,

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:15.000
can have large impacts
in the world around them,

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:17.250
especially in the ways
that they approach problems

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:18.780
that they're solving.

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:20.550
Is that doing it pseudo justice?

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:21.990
I know it's probably not
doing it full justice.

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:24.150
But could you expand a little
bit more on that, Vukosi?

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:25.983
VUKOSI MARIVATE: It was
a prize to recognize

00:17:25.983 --> 00:17:27.359
innovation using
machine learning

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:28.890
AI across the continent.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:31.200
So whether there was a
group or an individual,

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:33.330
it didn't matter, but
we wanted to see what

00:17:33.330 --> 00:17:34.630
people were doing out there.

00:17:34.630 --> 00:17:38.580
I think Yasini completely
embodies this, where you go off

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:42.180
and you have this societal
challenge that is out there.

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:44.910
And you think about, what
is an approach that I could

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:46.240
use to try and tackle this?

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:46.740
Right?

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:48.156
And then you go
backwards and then

00:17:48.156 --> 00:17:50.820
you find something
that's kind of tangible.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:54.270
And then you say, OK, I
need to now use this tool

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:56.790
and learn and see how I
can innovate around it

00:17:56.790 --> 00:18:00.000
to be able to then kind of
get people where they are.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.870
As opposed to going
like, you know, hey,

00:18:01.870 --> 00:18:03.690
I've got this hammer,
I need to hit nails.

00:18:03.690 --> 00:18:04.939
And I'm looking out for nails.

00:18:04.939 --> 00:18:07.500
And I think, yeah, that's what
the Maathai Award is about.

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:08.850
MELANIE WARRICK: So let's
talk about what you're

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:10.070
working on at TsogoloTech.

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:11.569
YASIN MUSA AYAMI:
Yeah, TsogoloTech.

00:18:11.569 --> 00:18:14.250
OK, so TsogoloTech
is a tech startup

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:16.950
that aims at making use of
state of the art technology

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:19.080
to kind of contribute
toward solving

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:22.230
these socioeconomic problems
that we're currently facing.

00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:25.160
So among them is the issues
of cholera, the issues

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:26.370
with financial inclusion.

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:27.930
Like underserved
communities, how

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:31.870
can we include them in the
formal financial sector?

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:34.320
What made me win the
Maathai Impact Award

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:36.680
was the app that
we created, called

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:40.030
Tukuka app, which aims at
automating transactions.

00:18:40.030 --> 00:18:43.260
What happens is, women
in underdeveloped areas

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:45.650
have resorted to coming
up with village banks.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:48.790
So village banks, this is
a kind of a system which

00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:50.925
enables people that are
living in the same area

00:18:50.925 --> 00:18:52.041
to contribute money.

00:18:52.041 --> 00:18:53.790
Then from that money
that they contribute,

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:57.120
then they are able to lend
it out amongst themselves.

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:00.330
And then they're able
to repay back interest.

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:04.050
So what was happening is, of
course, that was a good idea.

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:06.870
But the challenge
was the transactions

00:19:06.870 --> 00:19:10.440
that occurred during this
process were done on paper.

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:15.430
So we kind of thought that if we
kind of automate this process,

00:19:15.430 --> 00:19:16.930
and once we automate
this process,

00:19:16.930 --> 00:19:17.970
then we will have data--

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:19.890
data we can use for many things.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:21.750
Among the things that
can be done with this

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:23.440
data is approach banks.

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:26.980
Because you find that these
areas maybe do not have a bank.

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:29.100
So you can go to a
bank and say, OK,

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:32.960
see these people are
making x amount of savings.

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:35.970
And I think these people need a
branch or something like that.

00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:38.463
Or again, since there's this
issue of cryptocurrency,

00:19:38.463 --> 00:19:41.880
the blockchain, that is
making waves on the internet,

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:44.640
I mean, how can we
innovators come up

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:48.720
with ways in which we can use
these technologies to kind

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:52.560
of aid these people in
managing their transactions?

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.600
So that's what this
app was all about.

00:19:55.600 --> 00:19:59.310
And we envisage to find
of, by 2019, to reach out

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:01.200
to about 22,000 women.

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:02.550
MELANIE WARRICK: That's awesome.

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:03.234
Well, why women?

00:20:03.234 --> 00:20:04.650
YASIN MUSA AYAMI:
OK, well, if you

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:07.920
look at the stats, financial
inclusion stats in Zambia,

00:20:07.920 --> 00:20:12.090
about 41% of adults are
financially excluded,

00:20:12.090 --> 00:20:14.250
and majority of which are women.

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:17.180
And most of these
are rural dwellers.

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:21.280
So that's the reason why are we
mostly looking at these people.

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:24.750
Because these are people who
may be going to door to door,

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:26.760
selling items, farming.

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:28.470
So the little that
they are making,

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:31.650
if it's not properly
managed, then they

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:33.310
end up having nothing.

00:20:33.310 --> 00:20:36.894
So I think that's what kind of
made us focus mainly on women.

00:20:36.894 --> 00:20:38.310
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's wonderful.

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:40.590
And the app itself, can you
tell us a little bit about that

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:42.630
and how it functions or
what kind of technology

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:43.660
are you using?

00:20:43.660 --> 00:20:44.535
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: OK.

00:20:44.535 --> 00:20:46.900
So we created a
prototype, a mobile app.

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:47.590
MELANIE WARRICK:
It's called Tukuku?

00:20:47.590 --> 00:20:48.270
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: Tukuka--

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:48.560
MELANIE WARRICK: Tukukaka.

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:49.976
YASIN MUSA AYAMI:
--yeah, so which

00:20:49.976 --> 00:20:52.140
enabled these people
to kind of enter

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:53.760
their transactions on this app.

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:57.090
And then obviously there
was some backend application

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:59.250
that was storing the data.

00:20:59.250 --> 00:21:03.510
So now what we observed is the
literacy levels are very low.

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:06.450
Also the financial literacy
levels are also low.

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:08.100
So you find that
there were issues

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:10.320
in terms of recording
these transactions.

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:13.320
And you find that somebody
cannot write or record

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:16.170
the transactions, so they had
maybe to depend on somebody

00:21:16.170 --> 00:21:18.480
to record for them transactions.

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:20.720
Consequently,
somebody might end up

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:23.310
maybe recording wrong
transactions or one is not

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:25.510
able to track their
own transactions.

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:27.250
So if we kind of
automate this process,

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:29.940
we thought this could
somehow, you know,

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:31.815
knock out some of these issues.

00:21:31.815 --> 00:21:33.690
So that's the reason
why we created that app.

00:21:33.690 --> 00:21:37.300
But since these people
have low literacy levels,

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:40.110
it's been difficult to kind
of make use of the app.

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:42.600
And looking at the
internet levels,

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:44.840
the penetration of
internet levels,

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:46.680
it's still not that much.

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:50.010
So we're thinking of now
integrating the SMS system.

00:21:50.010 --> 00:21:53.070
So where we want to
use USSD for them

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:54.720
to record these
transactions instead

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:56.250
of using the mobile app.

00:21:56.250 --> 00:22:00.060
Then subsequently, after
we've got these transactions

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:04.230
automated, we're looking at
making use of state-of-the-art

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:07.860
machine learning, deep learning
to kind of help these people

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:09.100
view their credit profile.

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:11.970
Because if you go to a
bank, obviously, they

00:22:11.970 --> 00:22:14.350
need some form of collateral
for you to get a loan.

00:22:14.350 --> 00:22:17.492
And now these are people that
do not have access to banks.

00:22:17.492 --> 00:22:18.450
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:20.190
YASIN MUSA AYAMI:
And of course, many

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:21.870
do not have access to loans.

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:24.380
Just as an example, I
don't know if you've

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:25.380
been following the news.

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:29.110
We've had like three infernos
in the last couple of years,

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:30.930
which happened, I
think, two months ago.

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:34.020
[INAUDIBLE] COMESA market,
which houses people

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:36.460
from the COMESA region.

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:40.220
So you've got people from
Tanzania, people from Malawi,

00:22:40.220 --> 00:22:42.790
you've got people from Zimbabwe.

00:22:42.790 --> 00:22:46.374
So there was an inferno there,
and people lost goods worth--

00:22:46.374 --> 00:22:48.290
MELANIE WARRICK: But
they lost their currency.

00:22:48.290 --> 00:22:49.690
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: Yes, yes, yes.

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:53.040
So you find that that was their
only source of livelihood,

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:54.784
and they couldn't
be compensated.

00:22:54.784 --> 00:22:56.700
They were looking up to
the government to kind

00:22:56.700 --> 00:22:59.530
of compensate them, but you
know, the government couldn't.

00:22:59.530 --> 00:23:02.400
And so these people have just
resorted to staying at home.

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:05.460
So Zambia being a country,
a third world country,

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:07.830
that is striving
to fight poverty,

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:10.050
I don't think it's
possible to fight poverty

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:11.430
if that's the case.

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:15.550
So we're hoping that if
the app is successful,

00:23:15.550 --> 00:23:18.150
then we can help them
view their credit profile

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:19.800
and then,
subsequently, link them

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:22.110
maybe to formal
financial institutions,

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:25.670
like the banks, where they
may be able to access loans.

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:27.540
But of course, you know,
with village banks,

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:28.623
they're able to get loans.

00:23:28.623 --> 00:23:31.550
But it's only a limited
amount of money,

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:36.280
because the contributions
is maybe $1 or $2.

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:40.410
So if somebody wants a
loan of maybe $1,000,

00:23:40.410 --> 00:23:42.990
so you find that maybe the
group cannot sustain that.

00:23:42.990 --> 00:23:45.330
The amount of savings that
they've got cannot service

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:46.020
that.

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:49.260
So if we build the credit
profile, then subsequently,

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:52.380
these people will be able to
kind of go approach the banks,

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:54.120
and then the banks
will be able maybe

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:56.140
to give these people loans.

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:58.283
So that's what we're aiming at.

00:23:58.283 --> 00:24:00.120
And obviously there's
been a lot of research

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:01.110
that's been going on.

00:24:01.110 --> 00:24:04.540
I mean attending the Indaba,
we're talk about 550,

00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:07.230
the majority of
which [INAUDIBLE]..

00:24:07.230 --> 00:24:09.960
So if you carry out
a random survey,

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:11.430
I don't think
there's maybe anybody

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:13.830
that's maybe working in the
financial inclusion space.

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:16.830
Or, to be specific, somebody
working on this village app

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:17.850
thing.

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:19.920
One of the reasons could
be the issue of data.

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:23.290
So we're hoping that, if
we generate this data,

00:24:23.290 --> 00:24:25.000
we can then push
it to the public,

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:28.546
so people can make use of it
and then do some research.

00:24:28.546 --> 00:24:30.170
Of course, we are
cognizant of the fact

00:24:30.170 --> 00:24:32.400
that yes, there are
issues of ethics

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:34.110
that have to be considered.

00:24:34.110 --> 00:24:36.130
So that's where we
are heading towards.

00:24:36.130 --> 00:24:38.130
MELANIE WARRICK: So in
terms of the data itself,

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:39.420
you said you're
storing it currently.

00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:40.740
What are you storing it on?

00:24:40.740 --> 00:24:42.948
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: Well,
we're just storing it online.

00:24:42.948 --> 00:24:46.880
We got a backend application
that is storing the data.

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:52.650
And of course, we are going to
use some APIs to kind of expose

00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:54.945
the data if we want to use
it for machine learning

00:24:54.945 --> 00:24:56.490
or something like that.

00:24:56.490 --> 00:24:58.140
MELANIE WARRICK: Is it an
app that exists currently or?

00:24:58.140 --> 00:24:59.250
YASIN MUSA AYAMI:
It's a prototype.

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:00.600
MELANIE WARRICK: Prototype, ah.

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:02.310
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: But it's
not yet on the market.

00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:03.670
I mean, we're just
testing it out.

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:04.050
MELANIE WARRICK: Got it.

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:05.341
What are you prototyping it on?

00:25:05.341 --> 00:25:07.934
Is it on like an
iOS or an Android?

00:25:07.934 --> 00:25:08.850
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: Yes.

00:25:08.850 --> 00:25:10.200
It is an iOS app.

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:12.060
We used some Microsoft tools.

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:14.085
I don't know if I'm
allowed to of market

00:25:14.085 --> 00:25:15.180
other people's product.

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:16.440
MELANIE WARRICK: That's OK.

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:19.077
Yes, you can totally talk about
what other programs you used.

00:25:19.077 --> 00:25:21.660
What are some of the ones that
you used on the Microsoft side?

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:23.326
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: I
was using Submarine.

00:25:23.326 --> 00:25:26.140
Submarine enables you to
create cross-platform app.

00:25:26.140 --> 00:25:29.460
OK, so with TsogoloTech
we don't necessarily

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:32.070
have in-house developers.

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:34.860
So mostly it's people
who have got time

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:38.252
and are willing to spend their
time working on social issues.

00:25:38.252 --> 00:25:39.960
So it was one of my
friends, [? Janin, ?]

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:41.780
that was actually
working on the app.

00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:43.200
We're actually corroborating.

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:45.360
I was doing minor
changes here and there,

00:25:45.360 --> 00:25:46.830
but she did most of the work.

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:51.000
And with me, I was focused
on creating the backend

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:54.690
and obviously providing
her with an API that

00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:57.734
would enable her to consume
for the app functionality.

00:25:57.734 --> 00:25:58.650
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:25:58.650 --> 00:25:59.825
What are your future plans?

00:25:59.825 --> 00:26:01.200
YASIN MUSA AYAMI:
My future plans

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:03.540
are to tackle as many
problems as possible.

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:05.340
Of course, offline,
we're talking

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:06.720
about the issue of cholera.

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:09.450
In the next three
months, there will

00:26:09.450 --> 00:26:13.380
be another disaster I
presume, because there's

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:17.130
nothing that has been done from
the last time we encountered

00:26:17.130 --> 00:26:18.900
this problem, the
issue of cholera.

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:22.620
So I'm trying to also kind of
see if we can tap into that

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:27.919
also, to see how we can
use existing data to reduce

00:26:27.919 --> 00:26:29.460
the number of cases
that are recorded

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:32.310
and subsequently reduce
the numbers of deaths

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:33.930
that are recorded.

00:26:33.930 --> 00:26:35.430
So those are some of the plans.

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:36.270
And yeah.

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:38.474
Also, to get as many local
developers as possible.

00:26:38.474 --> 00:26:39.390
Because, I don't know.

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:42.630
I don't see it appropriate
for local developers

00:26:42.630 --> 00:26:47.190
to kind of contribute to
offshore projects on GitHub

00:26:47.190 --> 00:26:48.810
when we've got a
lot of issues that

00:26:48.810 --> 00:26:50.960
have to be tackled in-house.

00:26:50.960 --> 00:26:53.070
At Tsogolo we are
fortunate to kind of

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:55.120
have two groups of people.

00:26:55.120 --> 00:26:57.150
One are engineers,
and others are

00:26:57.150 --> 00:27:00.040
people that are working
on developmental work.

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:01.950
So these people working
on developmental work

00:27:01.950 --> 00:27:06.060
kind of point us to areas
which need attention.

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:08.820
They tell us, OK, this, I
think this needs a problem.

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:11.730
So we engage the engineers
to kind of come up

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:15.460
with a tech solution that
can tackle this problem.

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:19.050
So we're hoping to have as many
local developers as possible

00:27:19.050 --> 00:27:21.847
helping towards
achieving this goal.

00:27:21.847 --> 00:27:23.430
MELANIE WARRICK: Do
you accept support

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:25.705
from external developers
that are not-- you know,

00:27:25.705 --> 00:27:27.330
is that something
that you do currently

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:28.289
or that you want to do?

00:27:28.289 --> 00:27:30.205
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: Well,
I think it's something

00:27:30.205 --> 00:27:31.620
that, yeah, we'd want to do.

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:33.840
You know, collaborate
with external developers.

00:27:33.840 --> 00:27:37.860
I mean, on Saturday
I'm actually leaving--

00:27:37.860 --> 00:27:39.980
I told you I'm
leaving for Germany.

00:27:39.980 --> 00:27:42.540
So with Germany, we're
going for a sit-in program

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:44.370
but it's more like an
incubation program.

00:27:44.370 --> 00:27:46.620
So they kind of walk
you through the stages

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:50.015
of developing a product
and, of course, releasing it

00:27:50.015 --> 00:27:51.030
to the public.

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:53.910
And of course, ways you
can manage that product.

00:27:53.910 --> 00:27:56.430
So it's basically
aimed at startups.

00:27:56.430 --> 00:28:00.100
And then seeing how they
can sustain the startup.

00:28:00.100 --> 00:28:03.150
Because what happens is
you've got a startup that

00:28:03.150 --> 00:28:06.150
is in existence for a
few months to years,

00:28:06.150 --> 00:28:07.742
then it just disappears.

00:28:07.742 --> 00:28:08.700
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:28:08.700 --> 00:28:11.610
Well, that's great about the
startup incubator in Germany.

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:13.920
And then, in terms of, if
somebody is listening to this

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:15.336
and they want to
help or they want

00:28:15.336 --> 00:28:17.700
to participate in terms
of what you're building,

00:28:17.700 --> 00:28:18.862
how would they do that?

00:28:18.862 --> 00:28:20.820
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: So,
they can reach out to us.

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:21.300
MELANIE WARRICK:
We'll definitely

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:22.185
have your contact information.

00:28:22.185 --> 00:28:23.440
We'll get that from you and
put that on our show notes.

00:28:23.440 --> 00:28:25.440
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: Yes,
they can reach out to us.

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:27.630
Because the issue is we've
not been able to do much,

00:28:27.630 --> 00:28:29.460
because we're just
relying on people

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:30.750
that are already working.

00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:34.050
So they can only work on certain
projects when they are free.

00:28:34.050 --> 00:28:37.800
If we could have, you know,
proper funding to kind of

00:28:37.800 --> 00:28:42.050
have in-house maybe data
scientists, software engineers,

00:28:42.050 --> 00:28:46.540
we will be able to work on
these problems more quickly,

00:28:46.540 --> 00:28:47.040
you know.

00:28:47.040 --> 00:28:48.916
Because the issue with
tech is like they say,

00:28:48.916 --> 00:28:51.040
the only thing with technology
that does not change

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:52.320
is that it's always changing.

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:54.290
Maybe today I might
be talking about,

00:28:54.290 --> 00:28:58.260
OK, we want to make use of
cryptocurrencies and blockchain

00:28:58.260 --> 00:29:00.340
to tackle this issue
of financial inclusion.

00:29:00.340 --> 00:29:02.020
Tomorrow there's something else.

00:29:02.020 --> 00:29:03.990
So once I'm still
working on this,

00:29:03.990 --> 00:29:06.280
then it becomes
in a way obsolete.

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:09.390
So I just have to stop,
because I feel demotivated.

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:13.620
Our goal is to come up with
ways in which we can quickly

00:29:13.620 --> 00:29:16.920
work on these problems and then
maybe deploy it to the public.

00:29:16.920 --> 00:29:19.560
And then see if challenges
that people are facing,

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:22.410
analyzing a way that
is solving the problem

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:23.730
that we're trying to solve.

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:26.220
Then also trying to
identify ways in which we

00:29:26.220 --> 00:29:27.792
can improve on the product.

00:29:27.792 --> 00:29:29.250
MELANIE WARRICK:
That sounds great.

00:29:29.250 --> 00:29:30.127
Well, thank you.

00:29:30.127 --> 00:29:32.460
Before we wrap up, what I
want to touch on is, you know,

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:34.500
what are some of the
key things that you're

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:36.957
taking away from the Deep
Learning Indaba for this week?

00:29:36.957 --> 00:29:38.790
What are some things
that you've experienced

00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:41.820
so far that have been really
valuable for you in terms

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:43.210
of what you've seen?

00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:45.460
VUKOSI MARIVATE: Just also
touching on what Yasini has

00:29:45.460 --> 00:29:48.070
been talking about, in my
general knowledge at University

00:29:48.070 --> 00:29:50.640
of Pretoria starting out,
especially in data science,

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:54.270
is to do data science that
comes from social [INAUDIBLE]----

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:56.250
so coming from social
impact as a space.

00:29:56.250 --> 00:29:58.680
So I'll be very happy
to like, you know,

00:29:58.680 --> 00:30:02.520
have more conversations about
how do we go from that data,

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:05.580
find ways to release
it in the proper way,

00:30:05.580 --> 00:30:07.940
or release it just
to be able to use.

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:11.370
And for us, a place like
the University of Pretoria,

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:13.740
being able to work with
students to actually build

00:30:13.740 --> 00:30:15.790
on the research in that space.

00:30:15.790 --> 00:30:18.240
Because yes, in one
part, there is data.

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:21.570
The other is experts
like Yasini over there.

00:30:21.570 --> 00:30:23.841
And this also has
permeated the Indaba.

00:30:23.841 --> 00:30:25.340
If you go in and
you see the posters

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:26.930
and you see what people
are actually presenting

00:30:26.930 --> 00:30:28.580
and the things that
they're working on,

00:30:28.580 --> 00:30:31.100
you kind of start
seeing threads of,

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:32.704
oh, this could
turn into something

00:30:32.704 --> 00:30:34.370
bigger if you could
get, like, you know,

00:30:34.370 --> 00:30:36.260
one, two, three,
four people together

00:30:36.260 --> 00:30:37.790
from these different countries.

00:30:37.790 --> 00:30:39.950
You know, we shouldn't look
at our borders and say,

00:30:39.950 --> 00:30:41.300
that's where we end.

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:44.690
I think the Indaba is trying
to reduce that distance

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:47.180
and allow people to
be able to work across

00:30:47.180 --> 00:30:50.390
and be able to mentor
other students,

00:30:50.390 --> 00:30:52.230
mentors professionals
across these borders.

00:30:52.230 --> 00:30:55.850
So I'm hoping that is a
thing that is going to go on.

00:30:55.850 --> 00:30:59.130
I've seen examples of people
doing this after meeting,

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:00.289
especially at our meeting.

00:31:00.289 --> 00:31:02.330
Not to, like you Yasini
was saying, advertising--

00:31:02.330 --> 00:31:04.790
at Data Science Africa
I did meet people

00:31:04.790 --> 00:31:07.820
last year, as a part of
my trying to cover as much

00:31:07.820 --> 00:31:10.370
of the continent as possible
and bring people in,

00:31:10.370 --> 00:31:11.887
just like we met.

00:31:11.887 --> 00:31:13.220
MELANIE WARRICK: We did, I know.

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:15.157
And I was saying
this offline, that I

00:31:15.157 --> 00:31:17.240
know about this conference
because of you, Vukosi,

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:19.280
because you were at a
conference that I was at

00:31:19.280 --> 00:31:20.330
and you spoke about it.

00:31:20.330 --> 00:31:22.580
And I immediately was like,
how do I get involved?

00:31:22.580 --> 00:31:23.788
VUKOSI MARIVATE: So, exactly.

00:31:23.788 --> 00:31:27.530
I was at Data Science Africa
trying to talk about the story,

00:31:27.530 --> 00:31:31.070
show about my research, but also
that we're having the Indaba.

00:31:31.070 --> 00:31:34.610
That led to, we have one of
our co-organizers, Kathleen,

00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:37.210
who actually I met at
Data Science Africa.

00:31:37.210 --> 00:31:41.150
I had a gentleman that I got to
then mentor this year working

00:31:41.150 --> 00:31:43.460
on disease modeling in Malawi--

00:31:43.460 --> 00:31:45.740
just having like a
few Skype meetings.

00:31:45.740 --> 00:31:47.810
It's not this-- like,
people think oh,

00:31:47.810 --> 00:31:50.290
mentoring means you're
spending half your--

00:31:50.290 --> 00:31:51.190
MELANIE WARRICK: Years
and hours and all day.

00:31:51.190 --> 00:31:52.220
VUKOSI MARIVATE: Yeah,
I wasn't doing that.

00:31:52.220 --> 00:31:54.680
But then trying to reduce that
and being able to end up--

00:31:54.680 --> 00:31:56.960
I've seen people like
Benjamin Grossman

00:31:56.960 --> 00:31:58.294
doing kind of the same thing.

00:31:58.294 --> 00:31:59.960
WILLIE BRINK: Yeah,
and maybe I can just

00:31:59.960 --> 00:32:03.680
add that Africa as a continent
has so many problems that

00:32:03.680 --> 00:32:06.030
are unique to the continent.

00:32:06.030 --> 00:32:09.740
And it is very important
for us to equip our students

00:32:09.740 --> 00:32:11.760
to be able to deal
with these problems.

00:32:11.760 --> 00:32:13.580
And hopefully the
Indaba also goes

00:32:13.580 --> 00:32:15.530
some way towards that goal.

00:32:15.530 --> 00:32:16.940
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:32:16.940 --> 00:32:20.104
And I know that the sessions
that are being held right now

00:32:20.104 --> 00:32:22.020
are being recorded, and
that will be released.

00:32:22.020 --> 00:32:24.590
Do you guys know when
that's going to be released?

00:32:24.590 --> 00:32:27.320
WILLIE BRINK: We were hoping
for very soon after the Indaba.

00:32:27.320 --> 00:32:30.062
Maybe over the weekend
or early next week.

00:32:30.062 --> 00:32:31.770
MELANIE WARRICK: Great,
and-- oh, and you

00:32:31.770 --> 00:32:32.910
were going to say
something else?

00:32:32.910 --> 00:32:34.534
VUKOSI MARIVATE: It
also, again, speaks

00:32:34.534 --> 00:32:36.920
to this ethos of sharing.

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:39.410
Whether somebody is
at the Indaba or not,

00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:42.235
should get as much of
the content as possible.

00:32:42.235 --> 00:32:43.610
So there's a lot
of work that has

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:47.360
gone into the practical
sessions, and all of that

00:32:47.360 --> 00:32:48.920
is on GitHub already.

00:32:48.920 --> 00:32:50.754
And people can go
interact with it, I think.

00:32:50.754 --> 00:32:52.545
MELANIE WARRICK: We'll
get the links for it

00:32:52.545 --> 00:32:53.504
and put those up there.

00:32:53.504 --> 00:32:54.920
VUKOSI MARIVATE:
I keep on saying,

00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:56.730
we're trying to
manufacture abundance.

00:32:56.730 --> 00:32:59.270
It's not about
reducing access, right?

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:02.240
It's increasing it, so making
sure that there's videos.

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:05.450
There's the practical sessions,
and the practical sessions also

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:07.320
all that content is available.

00:33:07.320 --> 00:33:08.900
Yes, sometimes it does go down.

00:33:08.900 --> 00:33:11.420
When it goes down, it's
because it's being improved.

00:33:11.420 --> 00:33:13.160
It's not that we're removing it.

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:14.710
And that's what
happened, we know,

00:33:14.710 --> 00:33:16.970
we switched off last
year's practicals.

00:33:16.970 --> 00:33:19.520
We then start getting messages
of people asking you, please,

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:20.780
can we have access?

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:22.880
We were actually just
waiting for the switchover

00:33:22.880 --> 00:33:26.380
to this year's practicals,
which are much better.

00:33:26.380 --> 00:33:28.970
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah, I've
heard wonderful commentary

00:33:28.970 --> 00:33:31.550
in people seeing the
practicals and having

00:33:31.550 --> 00:33:32.980
wonderful experiences with them.

00:33:32.980 --> 00:33:36.320
And next year, Deep Learning
Indaba is going to be in Kenya.

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:37.210
Do you know where?

00:33:37.210 --> 00:33:39.844
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: Kenyatta
University in Nairobi, Kenya.

00:33:39.844 --> 00:33:40.760
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:33:40.760 --> 00:33:41.600
VUKOSI MARIVATE: The
good news is also

00:33:41.600 --> 00:33:44.360
that we are again trying to
increase the numbers quite

00:33:44.360 --> 00:33:45.520
substantially.

00:33:45.520 --> 00:33:48.440
So please look out for
the calls for applications

00:33:48.440 --> 00:33:49.930
for next year's edition.

00:33:49.930 --> 00:33:51.530
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:33:51.530 --> 00:33:54.200
Anything else that you wanted
to talk about or make sure

00:33:54.200 --> 00:33:55.740
we cover before we go?

00:33:55.740 --> 00:33:57.156
YASIN MUSA AYAMI:
So I just wanted

00:33:57.156 --> 00:33:59.450
to add on what we and
Vukosi were talking about.

00:33:59.450 --> 00:33:59.860
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes, please.

00:33:59.860 --> 00:34:01.970
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: Issues
to do with the problems

00:34:01.970 --> 00:34:03.560
that we're facing
as a continent.

00:34:03.560 --> 00:34:06.380
So one problem that
we're facing as Africans

00:34:06.380 --> 00:34:11.179
is, when we get people like
Google, when they approach us,

00:34:11.179 --> 00:34:13.690
so we leave Africa and
then go stay in Europe.

00:34:13.690 --> 00:34:17.429
In terms of making progress,
in a way, we're regressing.

00:34:17.429 --> 00:34:20.989
So we're not making any
changes to whatever problems

00:34:20.989 --> 00:34:22.170
that we're facing.

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:25.190
So I think of late,
yes, we've had

00:34:25.190 --> 00:34:27.020
issues where we've
had to hire experts

00:34:27.020 --> 00:34:30.290
from Europe to come and solve
our problems here in Africa.

00:34:30.290 --> 00:34:32.942
And of course, I don't know
for lack of [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:32.942 --> 00:34:34.150
I would say I know some more.

00:34:34.150 --> 00:34:36.350
And to say that, OK, we've got--

00:34:36.350 --> 00:34:38.507
there's unemployment in Africa.

00:34:38.507 --> 00:34:40.340
On the other hand,
you're outsourcing people

00:34:40.340 --> 00:34:41.989
to work for your people.

00:34:41.989 --> 00:34:46.400
So I think right now, since
AI is still in its infancy,

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:51.290
if we can get as much people as
possible into this space of AI,

00:34:51.290 --> 00:34:54.659
so that instead of outsourcing
people from Europe to come

00:34:54.659 --> 00:34:58.310
and solve our issues, I think
we should be the better people

00:34:58.310 --> 00:35:00.850
to kind of tackle our issues.

00:35:00.850 --> 00:35:02.780
I think we understand
them better.

00:35:02.780 --> 00:35:05.370
Like we all know, you know,
you cannot compare an original

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:08.300
vision to a translated vision.

00:35:08.300 --> 00:35:10.430
So I'd like to
recommend the ways

00:35:10.430 --> 00:35:14.180
that these guys like
[? Shakir ?] and other guys

00:35:14.180 --> 00:35:17.240
that are working in London.

00:35:17.240 --> 00:35:19.230
You know, of course
they got jobs there,

00:35:19.230 --> 00:35:22.070
but they did not forget
about this African continent.

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:26.000
And of course, decided to come
up with Deep Learning Indaba,

00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:29.990
and of course sacrificing
their time and effort in trying

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:31.790
to make this thing a success.

00:35:31.790 --> 00:35:34.310
From these 550
participants, you can just

00:35:34.310 --> 00:35:36.980
imagine how many more
people are going to benefit.

00:35:36.980 --> 00:35:39.320
Obviously, people are going
back to their communities

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:40.160
with feedback.

00:35:40.160 --> 00:35:42.260
And of course, the
Deep Learning IndabaXs,

00:35:42.260 --> 00:35:45.480
I think it was in 13
countries, the previous ones.

00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:47.970
So we've got about
22 African countries.

00:35:47.970 --> 00:35:51.480
So I'm hoping that in 22
African countries plus

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:53.770
there will be some
sort of an IndabaX.

00:35:53.770 --> 00:35:57.650
And then if the IndabaX is
yearly held around March,

00:35:57.650 --> 00:36:01.690
if we can have like, total
participants for these IndabaX

00:36:01.690 --> 00:36:03.170
even more than 100,000.

00:36:03.170 --> 00:36:05.750
So I think it will be
a great plus to meet.

00:36:05.750 --> 00:36:07.439
So like we will just
say that, OK, they

00:36:07.439 --> 00:36:09.230
are trying to increase
the number of people

00:36:09.230 --> 00:36:12.272
that are going to participate
in the Deep Learning Indaba.

00:36:12.272 --> 00:36:13.730
So people doing
this certainly need

00:36:13.730 --> 00:36:16.490
to attend the Deep Learning
Indaba, the main one.

00:36:16.490 --> 00:36:19.410
But through these small
Deep Learning IndabaXs,

00:36:19.410 --> 00:36:22.380
because of course there's
issues of funding.

00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:26.090
So you have to fly people from
Cameroon, people from Egypt,

00:36:26.090 --> 00:36:27.380
to come and attend Indaba.

00:36:27.380 --> 00:36:30.320
So that, in a way,
would kind of limit you

00:36:30.320 --> 00:36:32.130
in terms of doing
certain things.

00:36:32.130 --> 00:36:34.970
So if we can identify
people that we

00:36:34.970 --> 00:36:37.720
can train promptly
and of course run

00:36:37.720 --> 00:36:40.460
these Indabas in
respective countries,

00:36:40.460 --> 00:36:43.730
I think that would
be more effective.

00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:46.310
So as I was organizing
the Deep Learning IndabaX,

00:36:46.310 --> 00:36:48.530
one of the issues that
I faced was the issues

00:36:48.530 --> 00:36:49.550
to do with speakers.

00:36:49.550 --> 00:36:53.470
There were few speakers I knew
of that could deliver talks

00:36:53.470 --> 00:36:57.070
at the Deep Learning IndabaX,
one of which is [INAUDIBLE]..

00:36:57.070 --> 00:37:02.010
He was invited to come and do
sessions and much tutorials.

00:37:02.010 --> 00:37:04.760
So if we can also beat
capacity in that area

00:37:04.760 --> 00:37:08.450
to increase the number
of people that can also

00:37:08.450 --> 00:37:11.570
flow this content to
other people, I think also

00:37:11.570 --> 00:37:13.830
would be heading towards
the right direction.

00:37:13.830 --> 00:37:16.670
So I don't know if we can have
Indaba just sort of designed

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:20.030
to train people, to become
sort of a trainer of trainers.

00:37:20.030 --> 00:37:23.210
Which, yes, you know, when
you talk about IndabaX Zambia,

00:37:23.210 --> 00:37:24.290
we know exactly that.

00:37:24.290 --> 00:37:25.640
OK, there's x, y and z.

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:27.650
Because one is having
an IndabaX in Zambia.

00:37:27.650 --> 00:37:30.650
But two is also
having people who

00:37:30.650 --> 00:37:35.420
are able to deliver content to
these participants and then,

00:37:35.420 --> 00:37:37.370
of course, having these
participants benefit

00:37:37.370 --> 00:37:40.864
from the tutorials that will
be given by these participants.

00:37:40.864 --> 00:37:42.155
MELANIE WARRICK: This is great.

00:37:42.155 --> 00:37:43.070
And thank you.

00:37:43.070 --> 00:37:45.080
And anything else
that you guys wanted

00:37:45.080 --> 00:37:46.122
to touch on before we go?

00:37:46.122 --> 00:37:48.038
VUKOSI MARIVATE: The
thing I've been parroting

00:37:48.038 --> 00:37:50.210
over the last few days
just over and over again

00:37:50.210 --> 00:37:52.580
is going to be that, yes,
we are getting to a point

00:37:52.580 --> 00:37:55.730
where we have to think
about, how do we grow?

00:37:55.730 --> 00:37:59.180
Scaling is definitely on
our minds as organizers,

00:37:59.180 --> 00:38:01.490
and trying to figure
out what's the best way.

00:38:01.490 --> 00:38:04.400
At the same time, we
aren't ignorant of the fact

00:38:04.400 --> 00:38:08.270
of a lot of other initiatives
that have been kicked off

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:10.610
or have kicked off a
little bit earlier than us

00:38:10.610 --> 00:38:13.640
or have kicked off
after we have started.

00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:15.140
Even here in South
Africa, there's

00:38:15.140 --> 00:38:17.510
a lot going on in this
week in this area in terms

00:38:17.510 --> 00:38:19.320
of machine learning.

00:38:19.320 --> 00:38:20.900
So just think about
the continent.

00:38:20.900 --> 00:38:24.116
So how do we come together
as this wider community?

00:38:24.116 --> 00:38:25.490
MELANIE WARRICK:
And be stronger.

00:38:25.490 --> 00:38:27.073
VUKOSI MARIVATE: And
be stronger, yes.

00:38:27.073 --> 00:38:28.060
It's not about us.

00:38:28.060 --> 00:38:32.150
That's a thing I'll keep
saying to my co-organizers,

00:38:32.150 --> 00:38:33.390
that it's not about us.

00:38:33.390 --> 00:38:35.180
It's going to be
about the community.

00:38:35.180 --> 00:38:37.790
And to do this, we're going to
have to come together and come

00:38:37.790 --> 00:38:40.970
up with a strategy on how we
bring in all these resources

00:38:40.970 --> 00:38:43.330
that we have, that might
be minor in the bigger

00:38:43.330 --> 00:38:44.870
scheme of things,
but we still have

00:38:44.870 --> 00:38:46.700
to optimize them
to deliver well.

00:38:46.700 --> 00:38:49.280
And through this, if we
can connect them together,

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:51.850
find ways for people to
move in and around them,

00:38:51.850 --> 00:38:55.315
it builds a much better,
stronger community of practice.

00:38:55.315 --> 00:38:57.530
And right now there's
International Data Week

00:38:57.530 --> 00:38:59.540
that will be happening
in Botswana in November.

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:03.380
I am hosting a session there
on data for good, specifically

00:39:03.380 --> 00:39:05.897
to try and find even more
people who work in it.

00:39:05.897 --> 00:39:07.730
Like, you know, creating
these honeypots of,

00:39:07.730 --> 00:39:10.300
people will submit some
abstract and then you'll go in

00:39:10.300 --> 00:39:13.850
and say, oh, we didn't even
know that people in your area

00:39:13.850 --> 00:39:14.570
are doing this.

00:39:14.570 --> 00:39:16.810
So maybe you should
also come in.

00:39:16.810 --> 00:39:19.950
And so that community
building still has to go on.

00:39:19.950 --> 00:39:22.040
MELANIE WARRICK: And let
me end on this point.

00:39:22.040 --> 00:39:24.080
If people are interested
in getting involved,

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:25.580
you know, whether
it's participating

00:39:25.580 --> 00:39:27.440
in the actual
conference, whether it's

00:39:27.440 --> 00:39:29.390
providing resources,
you know, whatever it

00:39:29.390 --> 00:39:31.260
is, how do they get in touch?

00:39:31.260 --> 00:39:32.990
Who do they get in touch with?

00:39:32.990 --> 00:39:34.365
VUKOSI MARIVATE:
At the moment, I

00:39:34.365 --> 00:39:37.760
think on the website we've
set up a link to try and get

00:39:37.760 --> 00:39:40.085
as much information about
researchers, practitioners,

00:39:40.085 --> 00:39:43.430
and professionals on the
continent in machine learning

00:39:43.430 --> 00:39:44.400
in AI and data science.

00:39:44.400 --> 00:39:47.360
So you can go and actually
sign up and submit a form.

00:39:47.360 --> 00:39:48.650
We're trying to keep track.

00:39:48.650 --> 00:39:51.230
We have a mailing list that
people can sign up for.

00:39:51.230 --> 00:39:53.336
They can send messages
through that in terms

00:39:53.336 --> 00:39:55.460
of for the community that's
connected to the Indaba

00:39:55.460 --> 00:39:56.350
and beyond.

00:39:56.350 --> 00:39:58.619
That's the ambition there.

00:39:58.619 --> 00:40:00.410
And through the website
there is a contact.

00:40:00.410 --> 00:40:02.390
And if you send
an email to that,

00:40:02.390 --> 00:40:04.670
they will then get in
touch with us directly.

00:40:04.670 --> 00:40:07.250
We all man-- all the organizers
actually man the email,

00:40:07.250 --> 00:40:09.094
so somebody will
respond to you quickly

00:40:09.094 --> 00:40:10.260
in terms of what you can do.

00:40:10.260 --> 00:40:12.410
So the website is like
the lightning rod,

00:40:12.410 --> 00:40:14.390
and you'll get all
this information there.

00:40:14.390 --> 00:40:15.348
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:40:15.348 --> 00:40:18.710
Vukosi, Yasin, Willie, thank
you all for joining me today.

00:40:18.710 --> 00:40:19.795
I really appreciate it.

00:40:19.795 --> 00:40:21.170
VUKOSI MARIVATE:
Thanks, Melanie.

00:40:21.170 --> 00:40:21.960
WILLIE BRINK: Thank you.

00:40:21.960 --> 00:40:23.126
YASIN MUSA AYAMI: Thank you.

00:40:23.126 --> 00:40:25.910
MELANIE WARRICK: Today, I'm very
happy that Andrea Bohmert is

00:40:25.910 --> 00:40:26.940
here with us.

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:30.529
And I have special guest co-host
with me, Nyalleng Moorosi.

00:40:30.529 --> 00:40:32.070
Who I know she's
looking at my notes,

00:40:32.070 --> 00:40:33.770
because I wrote down her
name and I didn't write down

00:40:33.770 --> 00:40:35.936
the right spelling, but I
wrote it so I can read it.

00:40:35.936 --> 00:40:37.790
Anyways, thank you
both for being here.

00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:39.860
ANDREA BOHMERT: Well, thank
you very much for having us.

00:40:39.860 --> 00:40:41.120
MELANIE WARRICK: You're
here because we're

00:40:41.120 --> 00:40:42.380
at Deep Learning Indaba.

00:40:42.380 --> 00:40:44.330
And you are here to
give us a perspective

00:40:44.330 --> 00:40:47.420
from the VC perspective,
in Africa in particular.

00:40:47.420 --> 00:40:49.879
Can you tell us a little bit
about you and your background?

00:40:49.879 --> 00:40:51.253
ANDREA BOHMERT:
Sure, absolutely.

00:40:51.253 --> 00:40:52.770
So as you can hear
from my accent,

00:40:52.770 --> 00:40:54.311
I'm not originally
from South Africa.

00:40:54.311 --> 00:40:55.430
I'm from Germany.

00:40:55.430 --> 00:40:57.980
After finishing university
there, I came to South Africa

00:40:57.980 --> 00:40:59.870
for an internship
and kind of stayed.

00:40:59.870 --> 00:41:02.040
So that's now 20
something years ago.

00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:04.400
So I've spent the my
professional life actually

00:41:04.400 --> 00:41:07.010
all in South Africa,
first few years working

00:41:07.010 --> 00:41:09.950
for a big corporate and
now, since 12 years,

00:41:09.950 --> 00:41:12.560
actually in the venture
capital investment space

00:41:12.560 --> 00:41:13.625
here in South Africa.

00:41:13.625 --> 00:41:14.750
MELANIE WARRICK: Thank you.

00:41:14.750 --> 00:41:17.570
Well, so what has the Deep
Learning Indaba conference

00:41:17.570 --> 00:41:18.920
been like for you so far?

00:41:18.920 --> 00:41:19.585
ANDREA BOHMERT: This
week was actually

00:41:19.585 --> 00:41:20.835
a very, very interesting week.

00:41:20.835 --> 00:41:23.450
I was unfortunately only able
to be here for the first day,

00:41:23.450 --> 00:41:24.530
for the opening day.

00:41:24.530 --> 00:41:26.330
Because at the same
time in Cape Town

00:41:26.330 --> 00:41:28.470
we have three other
conferences kind of happening.

00:41:28.470 --> 00:41:30.540
So I'm actually very,
very excited to be here.

00:41:30.540 --> 00:41:33.810
I must say, so far I've
been very, very impressed

00:41:33.810 --> 00:41:34.560
with a few things.

00:41:34.560 --> 00:41:36.944
First of all, the sheer
quantity of people.

00:41:36.944 --> 00:41:39.110
The whole diversity, the
fact that there are people,

00:41:39.110 --> 00:41:40.850
I think, from 33 countries here.

00:41:40.850 --> 00:41:43.370
And very honestly, I
was quite impressed

00:41:43.370 --> 00:41:44.932
by the number of females.

00:41:44.932 --> 00:41:46.640
Normally one kind of
thinks data science,

00:41:46.640 --> 00:41:50.120
that this is still a very,
very heavily male dominated.

00:41:50.120 --> 00:41:52.190
And I was very
positively surprised.

00:41:52.190 --> 00:41:54.720
Quite a few women
actually are here.

00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:57.350
And I love the communication,
the level of communication.

00:41:57.350 --> 00:41:59.810
I thought that data
scientists are introverts.

00:41:59.810 --> 00:42:00.860
Apparently they're not.

00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:01.910
They actually talk.

00:42:01.910 --> 00:42:03.590
MELANIE WARRICK:
We are introverts.

00:42:03.590 --> 00:42:04.085
NYALLENG MOOROSI: Some.

00:42:04.085 --> 00:42:05.060
MELANIE WARRICK: Some of us are.

00:42:05.060 --> 00:42:06.268
NYALLENG MOOROSI: Yeah, some.

00:42:06.268 --> 00:42:09.050
I actually think we might
have a mix of some extroverts

00:42:09.050 --> 00:42:09.980
and introverts.

00:42:09.980 --> 00:42:11.600
But I think we
work well together.

00:42:11.600 --> 00:42:12.510
ANDREA BOHMERT: I thought
everybody would just

00:42:12.510 --> 00:42:14.370
stand there looking
down being very quiet.

00:42:14.370 --> 00:42:14.780
NYALLENG MOOROSI: All geeky.

00:42:14.780 --> 00:42:15.530
ANDREA BOHMERT:
And I walked around

00:42:15.530 --> 00:42:17.510
and everybody was chatting,
so I think that's amazing.

00:42:17.510 --> 00:42:18.230
That's great.

00:42:18.230 --> 00:42:18.860
MELANIE WARRICK:
My understanding

00:42:18.860 --> 00:42:20.360
is that you are
one of the biggest

00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:21.820
investors in AI in Africa.

00:42:21.820 --> 00:42:22.980
Is that statement correct?

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:24.980
ANDREA BOHMERT: Well, so
let's put it like this.

00:42:24.980 --> 00:42:29.040
I think we are one of
the oldest VC funds here.

00:42:29.040 --> 00:42:31.340
And yes, we have always
done tech investments,

00:42:31.340 --> 00:42:34.760
and we have done an
investment in a AI company.

00:42:34.760 --> 00:42:37.070
I think it's quite
interesting, because not

00:42:37.070 --> 00:42:41.756
many African investors have so
far invested in AI or machine

00:42:41.756 --> 00:42:42.880
learning kind of companies.

00:42:42.880 --> 00:42:44.930
A number of international have.

00:42:44.930 --> 00:42:47.780
If I want to talk about I'll be
one of the very few Africans,

00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:49.400
like South African
investors, who

00:42:49.400 --> 00:42:50.720
have invested in a AI company.

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:51.920
Then yes, absolutely.

00:42:51.920 --> 00:42:53.000
NYALLENG MOOROSI: You know,
you mentioned something

00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:55.250
about the number of
women that you seen here.

00:42:55.250 --> 00:42:58.790
How is the number
of women in the VC,

00:42:58.790 --> 00:43:02.360
especially in the area of
investing in AI in Africa?

00:43:02.360 --> 00:43:04.540
Are you just the one data point?

00:43:04.540 --> 00:43:05.700
ANDREA BOHMERT: Yeah.

00:43:05.700 --> 00:43:06.460
Yes.

00:43:06.460 --> 00:43:08.670
No, I think I'm not
the one data point.

00:43:08.670 --> 00:43:10.070
But I think that--

00:43:10.070 --> 00:43:12.100
I can actually not
talk about the African.

00:43:12.100 --> 00:43:13.940
I have to refer to
the South African.

00:43:13.940 --> 00:43:18.410
But I think in South Africa
there are maybe three, four

00:43:18.410 --> 00:43:21.290
female investment partners.

00:43:21.290 --> 00:43:24.530
Yes, it's a very heavily
male dominated world.

00:43:24.530 --> 00:43:26.542
But it is [INAUDIBLE]
and we are working on it.

00:43:26.542 --> 00:43:28.250
MELANIE WARRICK: From
what you're saying,

00:43:28.250 --> 00:43:32.420
why is it that not as many
VCs in the African continent

00:43:32.420 --> 00:43:34.399
are doing investments
in AI in Africa?

00:43:34.399 --> 00:43:35.940
ANDREA BOHMERT: I
think first of all,

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:38.225
you should invest in
things that you understand.

00:43:38.225 --> 00:43:41.319
And before you ask me
whether I understand AI,

00:43:41.319 --> 00:43:42.360
that's a different story.

00:43:42.360 --> 00:43:45.140
But I think a lot of people
are a little bit scared

00:43:45.140 --> 00:43:48.450
because, AI is kind of used
in close to every pitch.

00:43:48.450 --> 00:43:50.440
There's a lot of hype about it.

00:43:50.440 --> 00:43:52.460
There's buzzword bingo
absolutely around.

00:43:52.460 --> 00:43:56.210
If I look at the business plans
that come across my table,

00:43:56.210 --> 00:44:00.070
I would say every second
has got a word like AI or ML

00:44:00.070 --> 00:44:02.450
somewhere in the business
plan, which doesn't mean

00:44:02.450 --> 00:44:03.260
that they're really doing it.

00:44:03.260 --> 00:44:03.350
MELANIE WARRICK: True.

00:44:03.350 --> 00:44:05.266
ANDREA BOHMERT: And I
think a lot of investors

00:44:05.266 --> 00:44:07.460
are scared to fall for the hype.

00:44:07.460 --> 00:44:09.380
Because the moment
of course the company

00:44:09.380 --> 00:44:13.406
is really an AI company, they
ask for much higher valuations.

00:44:13.406 --> 00:44:14.780
So that's why a
lot of businesses

00:44:14.780 --> 00:44:16.988
are now trying to get to go
and sneak those words in,

00:44:16.988 --> 00:44:19.970
so they employ one data
scientist who maybe actually

00:44:19.970 --> 00:44:23.000
just does a bit of very
advanced Excel modeling,

00:44:23.000 --> 00:44:25.680
and they call it
now, we're doing AI.

00:44:25.680 --> 00:44:28.460
And I think a lot of
investors are still

00:44:28.460 --> 00:44:30.650
quite nervous to understand,
is this now a company

00:44:30.650 --> 00:44:32.620
that really knows what
they're talking about?

00:44:32.620 --> 00:44:36.200
Is this now really a company
that applies deep algorithms

00:44:36.200 --> 00:44:37.695
and understands
what they're doing?

00:44:37.695 --> 00:44:39.445
Or is it just one of
those hype companies?

00:44:39.445 --> 00:44:41.600
And I think that's one of
the big reasons for it.

00:44:41.600 --> 00:44:44.720
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, what does
AI mean to you as an investor?

00:44:44.720 --> 00:44:48.290
ANDREA BOHMERT: For me, it
means that you're not just

00:44:48.290 --> 00:44:50.396
slicing and dicing
existing kind of data.

00:44:50.396 --> 00:44:51.770
But actually that
you can kind of

00:44:51.770 --> 00:44:55.427
go and start using this data,
teach it to kind of go and get

00:44:55.427 --> 00:44:57.260
information out of
itself and just taking it

00:44:57.260 --> 00:44:59.450
to the next level to
come up with insights

00:44:59.450 --> 00:45:01.590
that were previously not there.

00:45:01.590 --> 00:45:05.630
And this is far more than just
slicing and dicing with data.

00:45:05.630 --> 00:45:07.380
It is taking it
to the next level.

00:45:07.380 --> 00:45:09.890
And for that you need to have
some very, very ingrained

00:45:09.890 --> 00:45:11.720
understanding and skills.

00:45:11.720 --> 00:45:14.840
And a lot of companies
simply do not have them.

00:45:14.840 --> 00:45:17.100
But they use the
buzzword without having

00:45:17.100 --> 00:45:19.920
the capabilities, without
actually applying it in the way

00:45:19.920 --> 00:45:21.235
that it kind of should happen.

00:45:21.235 --> 00:45:23.610
I think that's where one of
the big, big differences are.

00:45:23.610 --> 00:45:24.600
NYALLENG MOOROSI:
Can you just walk

00:45:24.600 --> 00:45:26.970
us a little bit on the
history of your company

00:45:26.970 --> 00:45:30.699
and how you ended up here
in this fairly unique space?

00:45:30.699 --> 00:45:32.490
ANDREA BOHMERT: In
terms of in South Africa

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:34.740
or in the Deep Learning
Indaba, or in terms of?

00:45:34.740 --> 00:45:36.150
NYALLENG MOOROSI: No, not
the Deep Learning Indaba.

00:45:36.150 --> 00:45:36.630
As a VC.

00:45:36.630 --> 00:45:37.050
ANDREA BOHMERT: As a VC.

00:45:37.050 --> 00:45:39.480
NYALLENG MOOROSI: In the idea
of the history of a VC company.

00:45:39.480 --> 00:45:40.355
ANDREA BOHMERT: Sure.

00:45:40.355 --> 00:45:42.570
How I got into VC is
actually quite a funny story,

00:45:42.570 --> 00:45:44.800
but might be kind of too long.

00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:47.820
I just wanted to be a VC and
there was no venture capital,

00:45:47.820 --> 00:45:51.750
so I managed to go and raise
a fund from a high net worth

00:45:51.750 --> 00:45:54.150
individual, the
previous founder of SAP.

00:45:54.150 --> 00:45:56.220
And that's how I
kind of got into VC.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:46:00.930
Our fund itself is now
about 12 years old,

00:46:00.930 --> 00:46:02.427
and it's quite interesting.

00:46:02.427 --> 00:46:04.635
In South Africa, we've got
a very, very established--

00:46:04.635 --> 00:46:07.290
actually, in Africa,
private equity industry.

00:46:07.290 --> 00:46:10.360
Everybody wants to
invest in infrastructure.

00:46:10.360 --> 00:46:12.690
But when it comes to
investing in technology,

00:46:12.690 --> 00:46:14.140
there's actually very little.

00:46:14.140 --> 00:46:16.650
A lot of people still say
that technology is too risky,

00:46:16.650 --> 00:46:18.150
early stage technology
is too risky,

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:20.490
and early stage
technology in Africa

00:46:20.490 --> 00:46:21.996
is definitely far too risky.

00:46:21.996 --> 00:46:24.120
So therefore, if you're
going to go and look at it,

00:46:24.120 --> 00:46:27.300
there is not much institutional
money, like in the US

00:46:27.300 --> 00:46:28.560
or in the world.

00:46:28.560 --> 00:46:30.840
The VCs are founded
by institutions.

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:33.190
In Africa, most of
them are actually

00:46:33.190 --> 00:46:35.490
founded by high net
worth individuals who

00:46:35.490 --> 00:46:38.180
are passionate about there's
enough to put certain money

00:46:38.180 --> 00:46:39.430
kind of into it.

00:46:39.430 --> 00:46:41.130
The problem is that
most of those funds

00:46:41.130 --> 00:46:42.820
are relatively small.

00:46:42.820 --> 00:46:47.870
So if you have a $20 million
fund in Africa, South Africa,

00:46:47.870 --> 00:46:49.650
this is a huge fund.

00:46:49.650 --> 00:46:51.700
Internationally, of
course, that means nothing.

00:46:51.700 --> 00:46:54.180
So one of the things
that is really,

00:46:54.180 --> 00:46:56.430
how to kind of go
and start making

00:46:56.430 --> 00:46:59.310
the right investments that
don't need $10, $15 million,

00:46:59.310 --> 00:47:02.460
but actually need $2, $3
million and really kind

00:47:02.460 --> 00:47:03.600
of go and take them.

00:47:03.600 --> 00:47:06.280
And we as a fund have done this
now for the last few years.

00:47:06.280 --> 00:47:09.000
We have just closed
our first fund,

00:47:09.000 --> 00:47:11.000
where we had seven investments.

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:13.980
Of the seven investments,
we had four exits

00:47:13.980 --> 00:47:17.922
to global kind of
Fortune 500 companies.

00:47:17.922 --> 00:47:20.130
We are one of the few funds
who can really go and say

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:22.620
that we have closed a
whole investment cycle.

00:47:22.620 --> 00:47:23.160
Investing?

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:24.520
Everybody can invest.

00:47:24.520 --> 00:47:27.000
The question is, can you
make returns out of it?

00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:28.680
And can you make
returns out of it

00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:30.576
that not just make you
as an investor happy,

00:47:30.576 --> 00:47:32.700
but make actually the
entrepreneurs and their teams

00:47:32.700 --> 00:47:33.810
happy as well?

00:47:33.810 --> 00:47:35.990
Because it is a
long-term journey.

00:47:35.990 --> 00:47:38.640
In venture capital investment,
you can't be short-term greedy.

00:47:38.640 --> 00:47:40.050
You have to be long-term greedy.

00:47:40.050 --> 00:47:43.230
It is a long, long time when you
have to kind of go in and work

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:44.240
together with companies.

00:47:44.240 --> 00:47:45.900
And yeah, we've done this.

00:47:45.900 --> 00:47:48.450
We've closed our first fund
very, very successfully

00:47:48.450 --> 00:47:50.220
on a high return.

00:47:50.220 --> 00:47:52.950
And now we are busy
investing the second fund.

00:47:52.950 --> 00:47:57.960
And yeah, I'm very, very excited
to go and see how AI and ML

00:47:57.960 --> 00:48:03.030
and how companies who are really
solving African problems using

00:48:03.030 --> 00:48:06.690
this kind of new insight and new
knowledge that actually exist,

00:48:06.690 --> 00:48:10.389
how we can go make some cool
investments in that space.

00:48:10.389 --> 00:48:12.430
MELANIE WARRICK: A couple
different questions are

00:48:12.430 --> 00:48:13.460
coming to my mind right now.

00:48:13.460 --> 00:48:15.430
But you know, one of
the things in particular

00:48:15.430 --> 00:48:18.160
that I know we talked about
briefly before was, how

00:48:18.160 --> 00:48:19.240
does this compare--

00:48:19.240 --> 00:48:20.710
startups that you're
investing in--

00:48:20.710 --> 00:48:24.130
compare to those that
you've seen out of, like,

00:48:24.130 --> 00:48:25.865
Silicon Valley, for example?

00:48:25.865 --> 00:48:27.110
What does that look like?

00:48:27.110 --> 00:48:29.693
ANDREA BOHMERT: So if you think
about it, as I just mentioned,

00:48:29.693 --> 00:48:31.550
our funds are relatively small.

00:48:31.550 --> 00:48:34.390
So if I can only go and
put $2 or $3 million

00:48:34.390 --> 00:48:38.090
into a company, the Silicon
Valley counterparts,

00:48:38.090 --> 00:48:39.940
they get $10
million, $15 million.

00:48:39.940 --> 00:48:44.890
Sure, we have a slight cost
advantage here in South Africa,

00:48:44.890 --> 00:48:47.432
but that only applies while we
are busy here in South Africa.

00:48:47.432 --> 00:48:48.931
So the moment you
want to kind of go

00:48:48.931 --> 00:48:51.220
into business international,
we still are traveling,

00:48:51.220 --> 00:48:54.020
everything still becomes
US dollar based and so on.

00:48:54.020 --> 00:48:58.130
So I think the big point is,
because our funds are smaller,

00:48:58.130 --> 00:49:00.850
therefore the amount
per company is smaller,

00:49:00.850 --> 00:49:04.270
our companies have to
become real businesses.

00:49:04.270 --> 00:49:06.410
That's an advantage
and a disadvantage.

00:49:06.410 --> 00:49:09.640
So we cannot go and take a
company that can go and be not

00:49:09.640 --> 00:49:11.840
profitable for the
next five years,

00:49:11.840 --> 00:49:14.015
who can just get and employ,
employ, employ people,

00:49:14.015 --> 00:49:16.390
maybe just go and be very,
very academic and come up with

00:49:16.390 --> 00:49:20.290
amazing results but don't
actually have a real underlying

00:49:20.290 --> 00:49:21.340
business.

00:49:21.340 --> 00:49:23.830
In Africa, in South
Africa, our businesses

00:49:23.830 --> 00:49:26.320
have to become very,
very quickly businesses

00:49:26.320 --> 00:49:28.540
that are making a profit,
because there is not

00:49:28.540 --> 00:49:31.294
enough money that you can go
and pump into them all the time.

00:49:31.294 --> 00:49:33.460
So therefore, there is a
slightly different mindset.

00:49:33.460 --> 00:49:35.470
If we compare the
companies that we

00:49:35.470 --> 00:49:38.410
invest in to their
competitors in the US,

00:49:38.410 --> 00:49:40.270
for example, then
we quite often see

00:49:40.270 --> 00:49:43.010
that they have sometimes
even bigger teams.

00:49:43.010 --> 00:49:44.340
They don't have case studies.

00:49:44.340 --> 00:49:46.240
They don't work on
return of investment.

00:49:46.240 --> 00:49:49.530
They do a lot of consulting
work but actually

00:49:49.530 --> 00:49:52.400
are not really worried
about, are we profitable?

00:49:52.400 --> 00:49:53.560
Are we making money?

00:49:53.560 --> 00:49:56.440
It's because there's not this
kind of pressure on them,

00:49:56.440 --> 00:49:59.200
because their investors will
just fund it and fund it.

00:49:59.200 --> 00:50:02.789
In our case we tell them, sorry,
you've got $2, $3 million,

00:50:02.789 --> 00:50:03.830
you have to make it work.

00:50:03.830 --> 00:50:06.290
At the end of that, you
need to be profitable.

00:50:06.290 --> 00:50:08.390
So our businesses
really, their focus

00:50:08.390 --> 00:50:10.390
is wide because they have
to employ salespeople.

00:50:10.390 --> 00:50:12.500
They have to employ
marketing people.

00:50:12.500 --> 00:50:14.140
It's not just about the science.

00:50:14.140 --> 00:50:16.184
It's actually about
building a real business.

00:50:16.184 --> 00:50:17.600
MELANIE WARRICK:
That makes sense.

00:50:17.600 --> 00:50:18.516
NYALLENG MOOROSI: Mhm.

00:50:18.516 --> 00:50:21.017
I think one of the things
that is always raised,

00:50:21.017 --> 00:50:23.350
especially when you get to
the space of entrepreneurship

00:50:23.350 --> 00:50:25.870
and obviously funding
entrepreneurs in Africa

00:50:25.870 --> 00:50:29.300
is that maybe the market or
the environment is not ready.

00:50:29.300 --> 00:50:32.020
I can see you maybe nod?

00:50:32.020 --> 00:50:35.230
Yes, this is exactly what
I would like to hear,

00:50:35.230 --> 00:50:38.530
is your opinion of,
what do you think

00:50:38.530 --> 00:50:42.040
is there in the environment
that either has enabled you

00:50:42.040 --> 00:50:44.950
and your company to
be able to capitalize?

00:50:44.950 --> 00:50:48.790
Or what do you think is there in
the market that actually might

00:50:48.790 --> 00:50:51.460
inhibit somebody else
coming in with their VC

00:50:51.460 --> 00:50:53.320
and investing in Africa?

00:50:53.320 --> 00:50:56.470
ANDREA BOHMERT: So I think that
all the various ingredients

00:50:56.470 --> 00:50:58.990
of the ecosystem
that is needed for

00:50:58.990 --> 00:51:01.420
successful entrepreneurship,
the ingredients are there,

00:51:01.420 --> 00:51:02.830
but they're not linked.

00:51:02.830 --> 00:51:04.840
They are still
working isolations.

00:51:04.840 --> 00:51:06.850
In some areas, in
some ecosystems,

00:51:06.850 --> 00:51:08.080
it's working closer together.

00:51:08.080 --> 00:51:10.900
In others, it's
definitely not, which

00:51:10.900 --> 00:51:13.090
makes it far more difficult.

00:51:13.090 --> 00:51:15.850
If you as an investor look
to kind of go and make

00:51:15.850 --> 00:51:18.940
investments, if you just wait
for the perfect business plan

00:51:18.940 --> 00:51:21.740
to arrive in your email inbox,
it's not going to happen.

00:51:21.740 --> 00:51:22.949
So you need to be out there.

00:51:22.949 --> 00:51:24.490
You need to kind of
go and understand

00:51:24.490 --> 00:51:26.920
that you will find
a rough diamond,

00:51:26.920 --> 00:51:28.480
and you know you
have to polish it.

00:51:28.480 --> 00:51:30.770
The perfect diamond
is not there.

00:51:30.770 --> 00:51:33.220
So you need to know how
to identify rough diamonds

00:51:33.220 --> 00:51:35.030
and you need to
understand how to polish.

00:51:35.030 --> 00:51:36.502
That's the first thing.

00:51:36.502 --> 00:51:37.960
From an entrepreneur
point of view,

00:51:37.960 --> 00:51:41.410
I think maybe because we
don't have enough highly

00:51:41.410 --> 00:51:43.620
visible success
stories out there,

00:51:43.620 --> 00:51:47.380
there's still not enough of
the real amazing potential

00:51:47.380 --> 00:51:50.020
entrepreneurs that
are actually deciding

00:51:50.020 --> 00:51:51.610
to become entrepreneurs.

00:51:51.610 --> 00:51:53.530
It's still quite
tempting to actually join

00:51:53.530 --> 00:51:55.655
some of the big corporates
who of course are paying

00:51:55.655 --> 00:51:57.170
much, much better salaries.

00:51:57.170 --> 00:51:59.890
And well, then life
is far more secure.

00:51:59.890 --> 00:52:02.839
And it's not that obvious of
how to really go and do it.

00:52:02.839 --> 00:52:04.630
I think our academic
institutions, and even

00:52:04.630 --> 00:52:08.600
our schools, are also not
preparing students early enough

00:52:08.600 --> 00:52:10.810
to also think business.

00:52:10.810 --> 00:52:12.880
I think we have a lot
of great specialists.

00:52:12.880 --> 00:52:14.463
But if you want to
be an entrepreneur,

00:52:14.463 --> 00:52:15.850
you also need to
be a generalist.

00:52:15.850 --> 00:52:17.744
You need to understand
how to manage people.

00:52:17.744 --> 00:52:19.410
You need to understand
a bit of finance.

00:52:19.410 --> 00:52:22.040
You need to understand a
bit of sales and marketing.

00:52:22.040 --> 00:52:24.580
So if you are purely a
specialist in a very narrow

00:52:24.580 --> 00:52:28.720
area, it's quite difficult
to go and run a business

00:52:28.720 --> 00:52:30.700
where you have to be
a generalist as well.

00:52:30.700 --> 00:52:33.010
And it's also trying
to kind of go and find

00:52:33.010 --> 00:52:35.740
the right diverse team,
right from the beginning.

00:52:35.740 --> 00:52:38.790
If you have a group
of 10 data scientists,

00:52:38.790 --> 00:52:41.699
they might come up with
the most amazing solutions.

00:52:41.699 --> 00:52:43.240
But I'm not sure
whether they come up

00:52:43.240 --> 00:52:44.820
with an amazing business.

00:52:44.820 --> 00:52:46.780
And I think so we
need to go and start

00:52:46.780 --> 00:52:50.320
learning that it's different
skills that are required.

00:52:50.320 --> 00:52:52.840
How do we as a country,
as a continent,

00:52:52.840 --> 00:52:55.810
make sure that entrepreneurs
actually have access

00:52:55.810 --> 00:52:59.200
to those skills or have been
taught those kinds of skills?

00:52:59.200 --> 00:53:02.240
And then all the other areas
of the ecosystem, the rules,

00:53:02.240 --> 00:53:04.480
the regulations, the
access to funding,

00:53:04.480 --> 00:53:07.140
all those kinds of things,
access to expertise,

00:53:07.140 --> 00:53:09.490
whether it's legal, accounting,
all of those things--

00:53:09.490 --> 00:53:12.310
all those things need
to kind of fit together.

00:53:12.310 --> 00:53:14.610
And then I think for the
rest I can definitely

00:53:14.610 --> 00:53:16.610
say that the
entrepreneurs that I see

00:53:16.610 --> 00:53:19.662
are absolutely on par
with what I see globally.

00:53:19.662 --> 00:53:21.620
It's the rest that needs
to kind of go and fit.

00:53:21.620 --> 00:53:22.230
MELANIE WARRICK:
That was actually

00:53:22.230 --> 00:53:23.580
going to be my next
question to you

00:53:23.580 --> 00:53:25.020
is that, based on
what you've seen

00:53:25.020 --> 00:53:27.330
so far in the
different countries

00:53:27.330 --> 00:53:30.240
that you've been to within the
continent, what's going right?

00:53:30.240 --> 00:53:33.240
What's working that's
making it a good place

00:53:33.240 --> 00:53:34.639
to be investing in AI?

00:53:34.639 --> 00:53:36.180
ANDREA BOHMERT: The
passion is there.

00:53:36.180 --> 00:53:37.570
The skills are there.

00:53:37.570 --> 00:53:40.440
Seed funding is often missing.

00:53:40.440 --> 00:53:43.230
The kind of, how to go and get
somebody to actually take this

00:53:43.230 --> 00:53:45.150
leap to actually
go and start it.

00:53:45.150 --> 00:53:47.490
Not everybody can
afford to not earn

00:53:47.490 --> 00:53:49.210
a salary for a year's time.

00:53:49.210 --> 00:53:52.560
So how can we kind of
go and close this gap?

00:53:52.560 --> 00:53:55.470
Access to market and
understanding how to sell.

00:53:55.470 --> 00:53:59.320
I mean, if your potential client
base is a big corporate, well

00:53:59.320 --> 00:54:01.110
then a lot of
people, particularly

00:54:01.110 --> 00:54:04.350
come straight from university,
they don't know how to do this.

00:54:04.350 --> 00:54:07.290
They don't understand how to
kind of go and commercialize

00:54:07.290 --> 00:54:07.990
the business.

00:54:07.990 --> 00:54:11.250
So where I see the
biggest challenge is not

00:54:11.250 --> 00:54:14.610
when it comes to, could they
develop an amazing solution?

00:54:14.610 --> 00:54:17.580
People here have a deep
understanding of the problems

00:54:17.580 --> 00:54:19.260
that they want to solve.

00:54:19.260 --> 00:54:21.030
They can develop the solution.

00:54:21.030 --> 00:54:23.580
What's quite often
missing is how do I

00:54:23.580 --> 00:54:25.510
take this solution to market?

00:54:25.510 --> 00:54:28.500
How do I get to go and
pitch to a big corporate?

00:54:28.500 --> 00:54:29.400
How do I go--

00:54:29.400 --> 00:54:31.350
what is the right kind
of pricing for it?

00:54:31.350 --> 00:54:34.050
So the challenge
is really in the

00:54:34.050 --> 00:54:36.030
how do I go and take
a fantastic solution

00:54:36.030 --> 00:54:37.969
and build a fantastic
business out of this?

00:54:37.969 --> 00:54:39.260
And I think we need to support.

00:54:39.260 --> 00:54:42.060
We need to kind of go and come
up with support structures.

00:54:42.060 --> 00:54:44.320
Because that is where
things are missing.

00:54:44.320 --> 00:54:46.580
And if I then go to
the US, for example,

00:54:46.580 --> 00:54:49.885
somehow I think that gets far
more ingrained from early on.

00:54:49.885 --> 00:54:52.010
There's different kind of
support structures there.

00:54:52.010 --> 00:54:55.830
So that just seems to
be a bit more easier.

00:54:55.830 --> 00:54:58.650
Maybe also because their
customer base is far more

00:54:58.650 --> 00:55:00.590
used to actually go
and speak to startups,

00:55:00.590 --> 00:55:03.720
whereas here our typical
corporates are still

00:55:03.720 --> 00:55:04.830
quite reluctant.

00:55:04.830 --> 00:55:08.520
It's typical, nobody ever gets
fired for buying dot dot dot.

00:55:08.520 --> 00:55:10.730
I mean, people here
are rather going

00:55:10.730 --> 00:55:12.590
to go into business
with larger businesses

00:55:12.590 --> 00:55:15.152
than to kind of go and give
a start up a real chance.

00:55:15.152 --> 00:55:16.860
So I think it's those
kind of things that

00:55:16.860 --> 00:55:18.111
quite often make a difference.

00:55:18.111 --> 00:55:20.151
NYALLENG MOOROSI: I have
two questions, actually,

00:55:20.151 --> 00:55:21.720
that just follow up from there.

00:55:21.720 --> 00:55:25.985
And the first question is, what
drives you to do this work?

00:55:25.985 --> 00:55:27.360
I can tell right
here that you're

00:55:27.360 --> 00:55:28.815
speaking with a lot of passion.

00:55:28.815 --> 00:55:32.230
But what drives you to
do it and to do it here?

00:55:32.230 --> 00:55:35.340
And then the second one is, what
are like the specific examples

00:55:35.340 --> 00:55:39.390
that get you very excited to
be investing in this space?

00:55:39.390 --> 00:55:40.890
ANDREA BOHMERT: I
come from Germany.

00:55:40.890 --> 00:55:43.740
I could go back to Germany any
day and start doing it there.

00:55:43.740 --> 00:55:46.170
But what I love
about this and what

00:55:46.170 --> 00:55:48.750
drives me is the fact
that, there are amazing

00:55:48.750 --> 00:55:51.270
challenges here, but
you can solve them.

00:55:51.270 --> 00:55:54.529
And you can make an impact,
and you can see the impact.

00:55:54.529 --> 00:55:56.820
It's in so many different
things, and I just love this.

00:55:56.820 --> 00:55:59.430
I love to work
with entrepreneurs,

00:55:59.430 --> 00:56:02.020
and sometimes one phone
call can make a difference.

00:56:02.020 --> 00:56:04.740
By helping with the
one little insight,

00:56:04.740 --> 00:56:05.982
it can make the difference.

00:56:05.982 --> 00:56:08.190
It can make a difference
not just to one entrepreneur

00:56:08.190 --> 00:56:10.010
but to the whole
team, to everything.

00:56:10.010 --> 00:56:12.190
So I love that part.

00:56:12.190 --> 00:56:14.070
I love the fact that
here you can still

00:56:14.070 --> 00:56:15.810
challenge the status quo.

00:56:15.810 --> 00:56:17.540
You can break new grounds.

00:56:17.540 --> 00:56:21.870
And I love the fact that success
can stimulate so many people.

00:56:21.870 --> 00:56:24.030
And here I see so
many young people

00:56:24.030 --> 00:56:26.764
who really want to go and make a
difference and that could also,

00:56:26.764 --> 00:56:28.680
with the right skills,
that could go wherever.

00:56:28.680 --> 00:56:32.430
But they choose to stay here
and build amazing businesses

00:56:32.430 --> 00:56:36.330
from Africa, to go and make a
difference, to be role models.

00:56:36.330 --> 00:56:38.080
And I think that's
what the country needs.

00:56:38.080 --> 00:56:40.080
And if I can play a
tiny little role there,

00:56:40.080 --> 00:56:41.430
I think that's amazing.

00:56:41.430 --> 00:56:42.420
That's the fun part.

00:56:42.420 --> 00:56:43.990
And I mean, I have
children myself.

00:56:43.990 --> 00:56:46.073
Yeah, I think if we want
to kind of go and prepare

00:56:46.073 --> 00:56:48.590
the next future for the world
that's lying ahead of us,

00:56:48.590 --> 00:56:51.090
well, you need to go and invest
in passionate entrepreneurs.

00:56:51.090 --> 00:56:52.320
That's just what I believe.

00:56:52.320 --> 00:56:53.820
MELANIE WARRICK:
Any resources you'd

00:56:53.820 --> 00:56:55.530
recommend to those
who are trying

00:56:55.530 --> 00:56:58.394
to start their own business,
exploring the space?

00:56:58.394 --> 00:57:00.060
ANDREA BOHMERT: I
think one of the tools

00:57:00.060 --> 00:57:02.550
that a lot of people
absolutely underestimate

00:57:02.550 --> 00:57:07.060
is networking,
networking, networking.

00:57:07.060 --> 00:57:09.870
If you just go and sit
behind your computer at home

00:57:09.870 --> 00:57:11.940
and you don't talk
to anybody else,

00:57:11.940 --> 00:57:14.887
you can read as much as you
want to online, and that's fine.

00:57:14.887 --> 00:57:16.220
But that's not going to make it.

00:57:16.220 --> 00:57:19.930
I think people need to go and
attend conferences like this.

00:57:19.930 --> 00:57:21.930
They need to go out and
they need to go and find

00:57:21.930 --> 00:57:23.640
the right communities.

00:57:23.640 --> 00:57:27.180
But also, push themselves out of
their comfort zones to not just

00:57:27.180 --> 00:57:30.700
kind of go with the like
minded, identical people.

00:57:30.700 --> 00:57:33.960
They need to kind of go and
find out who else is out there.

00:57:33.960 --> 00:57:36.670
So if you are somebody who
is very, very good at data

00:57:36.670 --> 00:57:38.550
science, you somehow
need to find somebody

00:57:38.550 --> 00:57:39.591
who's good at commercial.

00:57:39.591 --> 00:57:40.650
Try to find those people.

00:57:40.650 --> 00:57:42.630
Try to find out
who else is there.

00:57:42.630 --> 00:57:45.480
I think people need to
kind of go and understand

00:57:45.480 --> 00:57:48.420
that people buy
from people, people

00:57:48.420 --> 00:57:51.570
invest in people, people
do business with people.

00:57:51.570 --> 00:57:54.930
And therefore, we
need to go and build--

00:57:54.930 --> 00:57:56.160
the human touch is important.

00:57:56.160 --> 00:57:58.160
I mean, you've got to go
and build closer, still

00:57:58.160 --> 00:58:00.950
wider networks of different
people who can kind of go

00:58:00.950 --> 00:58:02.867
and engage, help each other.

00:58:02.867 --> 00:58:05.200
Because then you can build
all together success stories.

00:58:05.200 --> 00:58:07.770
So I think it's not so
much about, what do I read

00:58:07.770 --> 00:58:08.420
and what I do?

00:58:08.420 --> 00:58:12.914
It is about who do I talk to and
how open am I to just hearing

00:58:12.914 --> 00:58:13.580
different views?

00:58:13.580 --> 00:58:16.550
Because that might just trigger
how to work together with

00:58:16.550 --> 00:58:19.130
and how to kind of go and
build long-lasting partnerships

00:58:19.130 --> 00:58:22.757
across boundaries, across
countries, across disciplines.

00:58:22.757 --> 00:58:24.590
Because I think that's
what's really needed.

00:58:24.590 --> 00:58:26.120
That's what makes
the difference.

00:58:26.120 --> 00:58:28.036
MELANIE WARRICK: Any
other last bits of advice

00:58:28.036 --> 00:58:29.840
that you wanted to
share with our audience?

00:58:29.840 --> 00:58:30.964
ANDREA BOHMERT: Just do it.

00:58:30.964 --> 00:58:32.720
I mean-- it really is--

00:58:32.720 --> 00:58:34.248
MELANIE WARRICK:
Brought to you by?

00:58:34.248 --> 00:58:35.372
ANDREA BOHMERT: No, no, no.

00:58:35.372 --> 00:58:35.730
[LAUGHTER]

00:58:35.730 --> 00:58:36.320
I think that--

00:58:36.320 --> 00:58:38.000
MELANIE WARRICK:
Which is not a bad one

00:58:38.000 --> 00:58:39.940
to be mentioning
right now, frankly.

00:58:39.940 --> 00:58:43.370
ANDREA BOHMERT: I think the
issue is, there's always

00:58:43.370 --> 00:58:44.685
a reason not to do it.

00:58:44.685 --> 00:58:46.810
If you're going to go and
want to overthink things,

00:58:46.810 --> 00:58:49.850
there is always a reason not
to start your own business.

00:58:49.850 --> 00:58:52.400
There's always a reason
to go and play it safe.

00:58:52.400 --> 00:58:53.150
But you know what?

00:58:53.150 --> 00:58:53.870
This is the time.

00:58:53.870 --> 00:58:55.530
This is the age.

00:58:55.530 --> 00:58:57.500
There are so many
opportunities out there.

00:58:57.500 --> 00:58:59.810
I think people should just
take it while they're young.

00:58:59.810 --> 00:59:00.814
Just do it.

00:59:00.814 --> 00:59:03.230
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, Nyalleng,
thank you for helping me.

00:59:03.230 --> 00:59:04.220
And Andrea, thank you.

00:59:04.220 --> 00:59:05.180
This was really wonderful.

00:59:05.180 --> 00:59:05.550
Thank you.

00:59:05.550 --> 00:59:06.620
NYALLENG MOOROSI: This
was really good, yeah.

00:59:06.620 --> 00:59:07.310
ANDREA BOHMERT: Pleasure.

00:59:07.310 --> 00:59:08.460
Thank you for having me.

00:59:08.460 --> 00:59:08.750
Thank you.

00:59:08.750 --> 00:59:09.600
NYALLENG MOOROSI: Yeah.

00:59:09.600 --> 00:59:10.975
MELANIE WARRICK:
Thank you again,

00:59:10.975 --> 00:59:13.850
Yasin, Vukosi, and Andrea for
all coming onto the podcast

00:59:13.850 --> 00:59:15.020
to talk to us today.

00:59:15.020 --> 00:59:16.509
Really appreciate it.

00:59:16.509 --> 00:59:18.300
Now it's time for our
question of the week.

00:59:18.300 --> 00:59:19.190
And the question of the week?

00:59:19.190 --> 00:59:20.750
Main takeaways from
the conference.

00:59:20.750 --> 00:59:22.458
And I'll go ahead and
I'll kick this off.

00:59:22.458 --> 00:59:25.460
From the standpoint of what
has been really impressive to

00:59:25.460 --> 00:59:28.825
me is to see how
much what I see here

00:59:28.825 --> 00:59:30.200
in terms of talking
to the people

00:59:30.200 --> 00:59:32.810
I've talked to is
common across the board.

00:59:32.810 --> 00:59:36.470
You know, the fears of I don't
know enough, I'm scared--

00:59:36.470 --> 00:59:39.540
the imposter syndrome,
which everyone has.

00:59:39.540 --> 00:59:41.180
But then still,
the people who are

00:59:41.180 --> 00:59:43.730
willing to take the risks,
to go into the space,

00:59:43.730 --> 00:59:45.140
has been really impressive.

00:59:45.140 --> 00:59:47.960
And what's even more impressive
to me about this conference

00:59:47.960 --> 00:59:51.320
is how welcoming and
inclusive people are.

00:59:51.320 --> 00:59:52.970
You don't always
see that everywhere.

00:59:52.970 --> 00:59:55.850
And this space can
definitely lead to a very,

00:59:55.850 --> 00:59:58.426
like, you don't have
enough x, y and z.

00:59:58.426 --> 01:00:00.050
Certain people will
have that attitude.

01:00:00.050 --> 01:00:03.080
But thankfully, everyone
I've come across here

01:00:03.080 --> 01:00:07.880
is just very eager to open
doors, just excited to learn,

01:00:07.880 --> 01:00:09.410
and that's really amazing to me.

01:00:09.410 --> 01:00:11.940
I'm so glad I was able to come
and see that and experience

01:00:11.940 --> 01:00:12.440
that.

01:00:12.440 --> 01:00:14.120
And I'm grateful
for you guys making

01:00:14.120 --> 01:00:15.370
it so easy for me to be here.

01:00:15.370 --> 01:00:16.370
So thank you.

01:00:16.370 --> 01:00:18.800
VUKOSI MARIVATE: I think for
me, my key takeaway really

01:00:18.800 --> 01:00:23.430
is a question, which
is, where do we go next?

01:00:23.430 --> 01:00:28.490
And I think as an organization
and as a community,

01:00:28.490 --> 01:00:31.460
we really have to think
very hard about where are we

01:00:31.460 --> 01:00:32.120
taking this?

01:00:32.120 --> 01:00:36.050
I mean, Africa has such a huge
appetite for machine learning,

01:00:36.050 --> 01:00:37.250
for AI.

01:00:37.250 --> 01:00:38.810
There is a lot of talent here.

01:00:38.810 --> 01:00:41.330
Africa, of course, has
all these challenges

01:00:41.330 --> 01:00:44.130
which are very unique globally.

01:00:44.130 --> 01:00:46.280
So I think we will
continue to discuss

01:00:46.280 --> 01:00:48.830
what is the best way
that we can really

01:00:48.830 --> 01:00:53.870
strengthen Africa and really
make the world a better place.

01:00:53.870 --> 01:00:56.260
NYALLENG MOOROSI: Yeah,
my takeaway from this

01:00:56.260 --> 01:00:58.790
probably is just a
lot of excitement

01:00:58.790 --> 01:01:02.840
because what differentiated last
year's Indaba from this year's

01:01:02.840 --> 01:01:05.630
Indaba is the leap
that the last year

01:01:05.630 --> 01:01:08.720
Indaba had on the audience.

01:01:08.720 --> 01:01:11.076
Because this time
around, what has happened

01:01:11.076 --> 01:01:12.950
is that people have gone
back and people have

01:01:12.950 --> 01:01:14.900
gone to transform their lives.

01:01:14.900 --> 01:01:21.020
And my most inspired moment
was during the Maathai Awards.

01:01:21.020 --> 01:01:24.860
Obviously, he's one of the
people that is on the podcast,

01:01:24.860 --> 01:01:27.500
and I think he is inspiring.

01:01:27.500 --> 01:01:32.540
So my takeaway from here
this week is, AI in Africa

01:01:32.540 --> 01:01:33.710
is happening.

01:01:33.710 --> 01:01:36.660
And AI in Africa
can have impact.

01:01:36.660 --> 01:01:40.020
And the impact, we
already see the results.

01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:43.310
So there is no real
need for us to doubt.

01:01:43.310 --> 01:01:45.440
Believe in the
bigger things that

01:01:45.440 --> 01:01:47.360
are proposed as challenges.

01:01:47.360 --> 01:01:50.460
Because for as much as
they are challenges,

01:01:50.460 --> 01:01:53.180
there are so many
examples now here

01:01:53.180 --> 01:01:55.880
that we have seen at
the Indaba of people

01:01:55.880 --> 01:01:59.120
that have ignored those
challenges and went forward

01:01:59.120 --> 01:02:03.830
and built AI and AI tools
to improve African lives.

01:02:03.830 --> 01:02:04.750
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

01:02:04.750 --> 01:02:06.720
